  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Dutch co-financing of African Emergency Food Production Facility grants to a wheat value chain development project in Ethiopia and a project that focu</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The $1.5 billion AEFPF consists of three key pillars: &#x2022;Delivering certified seeds of climateadapted varieties, fertilizers, and extension services to 20 million farmers; and support post-harvest management and market development. Where possible, the program will promote the use of innovation and ICT platforms (such as e-wallets) for delivery of these inputs and technology to farmers. &#x2022;Providing financing for the large-scale supply of fertilizers to wholesalers and aggregators to bridge the two million MT supply gap in fertilizer, Under Pillar II, financing for the fertilizer value chain under AEFPF will be sovereign operations that meet the immediate liquidity requirements for RMCs to procure fertilizer. &#x2022;Promoting policy dialogue and the implementation of policy reforms to address structural factors that are preventing modern inputs from reaching farmers in short and medium term. For example, by prioritizing policy actions and measures designed to strengthen national institutions that oversee input markets and policy commitments</narrative>
  </description>
